WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.437
You have the spec, the precise definition of what you want.

00:00:05.437 --> 00:00:08.873
Now the model writes the SQL.

00:00:08.873 --> 00:00:12.309
The first attempt rarely lands.

00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:15.746
It names a column that has since been renamed, or it joins two tables on the wrong key.

00:00:15.746 --> 00:00:19.183
A one shot model has no way to know any of this.

00:00:19.183 --> 00:00:22.619
It just ships the broken query and moves on.

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:28.322
So you give the model a real database to run against, reached through a tool like MCP or a CLI.

00:00:28.322 --> 00:00:34.025
Now it writes a query, runs it, reads the error the database hands back, and rewrites.

00:00:34.025 --> 00:00:39.727
Then it does that again, in seconds, many times over.

00:00:39.727 --> 00:00:45.430
This is the same loop you run by hand when you debug, just automated.

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:51.133
It is exactly the difference between one shot and multi shot, and it is most of the jump in those benchmark scores.

00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:55.828
The database does a lot of checking for you, and for free.

00:00:55.828 --> 00:01:00.522
A column that does not exist.

00:01:00.522 --> 00:01:05.217
A type that does not match.

00:01:05.217 --> 00:01:09.912
A join that comes back with zero rows.

00:01:09.912 --> 00:01:14.606
Each one is an error the engine raises the instant it runs the query, and the model reads that error and fixes it, with no human in the loop.

00:01:14.606 --> 00:01:19.301
Everything mechanical, the engine catches on its own.

00:01:19.301 --> 00:01:23.995
But the database has no opinion about what you meant.

00:01:23.995 --> 00:01:28.690
Ask for active users, and the model can write a query that runs, returns a clean number, and counts the wrong thing, because active user has a dozen definitions and it quietly guessed one.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:33.385
That query sails straight through execution.

00:01:33.385 --> 00:01:38.079
The engine only ran what it was given.

00:01:38.079 --> 00:01:42.774
A query that runs is not yet a query that is right, and catching that is the next step.

00:01:42.774 --> 00:01:46.805
So execution earns you a query that works, and it earns it the moment the database runs the query.

00:01:46.805 --> 00:01:50.836
The crashes are caught.

00:01:50.836 --> 00:01:54.867
The empty results are caught.

00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:58.898
The wrong but running answer is not, because the database ran what it was given and cannot read your mind.

00:01:58.898 --> 00:02:02.929
Proving that one is the final step: verification.
